WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.319
Optimistic concurrency allows competing
work and detects when

2
00:00:03.319 --> 00:00:06.315
an update is based on an obsolete version.

3
00:00:06.315 --> 00:00:09.663
It is useful for document editing,
configuration changes, and

4
00:00:09.663 --> 00:00:13.011
other operations where collisions are
possible but relatively uncommon.

5
00:00:13.011 --> 00:00:16.007
It does not prevent every writer from
starting.

6
00:00:16.007 --> 00:00:18.650
A record carries a version or revision.

7
00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:21.469
A caller reads version twelve and submits
an

8
00:00:21.469 --> 00:00:24.465
update conditional on that version still
being current.

9
00:00:24.465 --> 00:00:27.989
The storage operation replaces the content
and advances the version

10
00:00:27.989 --> 00:00:31.425
together, using the expected version in
its mutation predicate.

11
00:00:31.425 --> 00:00:34.333
If another writer already changed the
record, the

12
00:00:34.333 --> 00:00:37.329
stale write cannot silently replace the
newer content.

13
00:00:37.329 --> 00:00:39.972
Comparison and version advancement must be
atomic.

14
00:00:39.972 --> 00:00:42.967
Reading the version first, writing
content, and incrementing

15
00:00:42.967 --> 00:00:46.316
the version in a separate statement
recreates the race.

16
00:00:46.316 --> 00:00:48.430
Every writer that can invalidate the

17
00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:51.073
snapshot must participate in the version
contract.

18
00:00:51.073 --> 00:00:53.893
An administrative script that changes
content without changing

19
00:00:53.893 --> 00:00:56.888
the revision defeats the check for later
writers.

20
00:00:56.888 --> 00:00:59.179
Inspect the affected-row or
conditional-write result.

21
00:00:59.179 --> 00:01:02.792
No matching update means the expected
state was unavailable; a

22
00:01:02.792 --> 00:01:06.844
conflict response may be appropriate after
considering deletion and access policy.

23
00:01:06.844 --> 00:01:09.311
HTTP APIs can expose a strong ETag

24
00:01:09.311 --> 00:01:11.955
and require If-Match for a conditional
edit.

25
00:01:11.955 --> 00:01:14.069
The server must enforce that precondition

26
00:01:14.069 --> 00:01:16.360
at the same authoritative mutation
boundary.

27
00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:18.475
Returning an ETag while ignoring it

28
00:01:18.475 --> 00:01:20.765
during writes provides no lost-update
protection.

29
00:01:20.765 --> 00:01:23.409
Choose conflict handling from the
operation's meaning.

30
00:01:23.409 --> 00:01:25.611
For a user-edited document, return a

31
00:01:25.611 --> 00:01:28.254
conflict and support refresh or deliberate
merge.

32
00:01:28.254 --> 00:01:31.074
Blindly reloading the newest version and
applying the

33
00:01:31.074 --> 00:01:34.422
same stale whole-object replacement may
erase someone else's work.

34
00:01:34.422 --> 00:01:38.034
For a recalculable operation, reloading
state and rerunning its business

35
00:01:38.034 --> 00:01:41.382
logic can be safe if its effects are
controlled.

36
00:01:41.382 --> 00:01:44.378
Merge only when the data semantics support
it.

37
00:01:44.378 --> 00:01:46.581
Optimistic checks detect interference;
pessimistic locks

38
00:01:46.581 --> 00:01:48.519
restrict conflicting access while held.

39
00:01:48.519 --> 00:01:50.722
Neither mechanism replaces authorization,
and retries

40
00:01:50.722 --> 00:01:52.660
still need an explicit policy.

